    {
      "type": "done",
      "answer": "A mutex protects shared state by providing **serialized access to a critical section**, which ensures **race avoidance** for the protected shared state. In practice, only one thread holds the mutex at a time; other threads must wait until the lock is released, thereby serializing the execution of the protected region. The standard pattern involves calling `pthread_mutex_lock(&lock)` before accessing the shared state and `pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock)` after the access is complete [S1].\n\nThe cost of using a mutex is **lock contention** [S1].\n\n**Source:**\n- Page 58 [S1]",
      "citations": [
        {
          "document_id": "34911c46e78edc833f283f8dd678b85d6f8c92d66859e6bf6886949181d95402",
          "chunk_id": "34911c46e78edc833f283f8dd678b85d6f8c92d66859e6bf6886949181d95402-p58-1",
          "page": 58,
          "text": "CPU Multicore Programming: pThread\n  Mutex: Protect Shared State\n\n                                              ¡ The Mutex provides:\n¡ Mutex pattern:\n                                                ¡  Serialized access to critical section\n      pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);\n                                                ¡ Race avoidance for protected     sum += local_sum;\n                                                         shared state      pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);\n                                              ¡  Cost: Lock contention\n\n\n\n\n\n      Only one thread holds the mutex; others wait and the protected region is serialized",
          "preview_url": "/api/documents/34911c46e78edc833f283f8dd678b85d6f8c92d66859e6bf6886949181d95402/pages/58/preview",
          "source": "S1"
